.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Doors API provides descriptor-capability remote procedure calls between
processes on one FreeBSD system.
.Fn door_create
creates a Door owned by the calling process, records
.Fa cookie ,
starts the initial managed worker, and returns a Door descriptor.
The worker invokes
.Fa proc
with the creation cookie, request data, and received descriptors.
.Pp
The callback normally completes the invocation with
.Fn door_return
and then returns normally.
The reply buffers are consumed before the callback frame is released.
.Fn door_return
must be called only from the active server callback.
.Pp
.Fn door_call
performs a synchronous invocation.
On input,
.Fa arg->data_ptr
and
.Fa arg->data_size
describe request data;
.Fa arg->desc_ptr
and
.Fa arg->desc_num
describe request descriptors; and
.Fa arg->rbuf
and
.Fa arg->rsize
provide reply storage.
On success, the data fields describe the reply and returned descriptor metadata
is owned by the caller.
If the reply does not fit in the supplied buffer, including when
.Va rbuf
is
.Dv NULL
and
.Va rsize
is zero, the kernel creates a page-rounded anonymous mapping, stores its base
and length in
.Va rbuf
and
.Va rsize ,
and makes
.Va data_ptr
refer to that mapping.
The caller must release a replacement mapping with
.Xr munmap 2
after consuming the reply.
.Pp
.Fn door_oneway
submits a data-only request to a Door created with
.Dv DOOR_ONEWAY .
The kernel snapshots the request before returning, but callback completion may
occur later.
.Ss Correlated asynchronous requests
.Fn door_async_open
creates a private completion endpoint for an ordinary Door or a preconnected
.Dv DOOR_ONEWAY
endpoint.
.Ss Kernel-routed asynchronous calls
On an ordinary Door,
.Fn door_async_submit
uses
.Dv DOOR_SC_ASYNC_CALL
and passes the request bytes directly.
The server uses its ordinary callback and
.Fn door_return ;
no token protocol or server registration is needed.
Requests are data-only, at most 1 MiB; replies are at most 64 KiB.
Each completion Door admits at most 256 unfinished kernel calls, including
raw syscall submissions. This is not a per-process kernel quota.
One terminal completion is scheduled for each accepted call; closing or
revoking the completion endpoint can prevent userspace delivery.
Status is zero with result bytes, or a positive errno without bytes:
revocation gives
.Er EBADF
and isolated worker disappearance gives
.Er ECONNRESET .
The raw header carries
.Dv DOOR_ASYNC_C_REVOKED ,
.Dv DOOR_ASYNC_C_WORKER_EXIT ,
or
.Dv DOOR_ASYNC_C_CANCELLED
as applicable.
.Fn door_async_cancel
uses the request and caller-owned completion capabilities to select
.Er ECANCELED
if cancellation wins. It does not stop an already running server callback.
.Ss Legacy ONEWAY request protocol
For a
.Dv DOOR_ONEWAY
endpoint, submit wraps bytes in a versioned request with a context token.
The application first uses an authenticated synchronous control Door to send
.Fn door_async_reply_fd
and
.Fn door_async_token
to the peer.
The control callback calls
.Fn door_async_server_register
while caller credentials are available and acknowledges registration.
The server verifies reply ownership, parses requests with
.Fn door_async_request_parse ,
and replies with
.Fn door_async_server_respond .
The registry selects the reply capability by token and checks captured process
and jail identity; its size is bounded by
.Fa max_sessions .
The low-level
.Fn door_async_respond
is for a dedicated authenticated connection.
Deferred application work must copy borrowed request bytes before callback
return; the parsed request retains only identity and credential metadata.
This legacy protocol supplies neither timeout nor remote cancellation;
.Fn door_async_cancel
returns
.Er ENOTSUP .
.Fn door_async_server_unregister
closes the registered capability. Arrange authenticated teardown and drain
request callbacks before
.Fn door_async_server_close .
.Ss Completion and context lifetime
Both modes return
.Er EAGAIN
at the context's
.Fa max_pending
limit or the additional process-wide libdoor limit of 4096 pending requests.
Kernel admission limits are separate.
Received completions run on private reply workers; stale/duplicate replies
are ignored. Bytes passed to a completion callback are borrowed only for its
invocation.
.Fn door_async_close
stops submissions, revokes the reply endpoint and drains its managed workers.
It invokes remaining pending callbacks synchronously on the closing thread
with
.Er ECANCELED .
Thread-affine runtimes must dispatch callbacks from either thread context.
Close is not a barrier for callbacks running at the request peer.
Call it outside a completion callback, after stopping other context and
borrowed-reply-fd users. Arrange peer-death detection and retire contexts when
the peer generation changes.
.Ss Creation attributes
.Bl -tag -width DOOR_NO_DEPLETION_CB
.It Dv DOOR_PRIVATE
Use a private worker pool.
.It Dv DOOR_UNREF
Deliver notification when the last client reference is released.
.It Dv DOOR_UNREF_MULTI
Rearm unreferenced notifications; implies
.Dv DOOR_UNREF .
.It Dv DOOR_REFUSE_DESC
Reject calls containing descriptors.
.It Dv DOOR_NO_CANCEL
Ignore ordinary signal and explicit cancellation while still allowing revoke
and lifecycle termination.
.It Dv DOOR_NO_DEPLETION_CB
Disable depletion callback behavior.
.It Dv DOOR_ONEWAY
Use asynchronous data-only submissions and imply descriptor refusal.
.It Dv DOOR_NO_RETURN
Optimize callbacks that return no data or descriptors.
.It Dv DOOR_CROSS_JAIL
Allow a host-owned endpoint to grant capabilities to other prisons.
.It Dv DOOR_NO_CRED
Do not capture client credentials; credential queries return
.Er ENOTSUP .
.El
.Pp
.Dv DOOR_PRIVCREATE
is output-only and must not be supplied to ordinary creation.
.Ss Descriptor transfer
For each descriptor sent in a call or reply,
.Va d_attributes
must contain
.Dv DOOR_DESCRIPTOR
and
.Va d_descriptor
must name an open descriptor.
The optional
.Dv DOOR_RELEASE
bit selects ownership transfer.
.Pp
Libdoor translates this public bit into the private kernel flag
.Dv DOOR_DESC_F_MOVE .
If
.Dv DOOR_RELEASE
is absent, the private flag word is zero and the receiver obtains an
independent duplicate while the sender retains its descriptor.
If it is present, the kernel consumes the sender's descriptor only when the
complete descriptor transaction and invocation have committed successfully.
Validation or dispatch failure leaves every source descriptor open, including
those marked for release.
.Pp
For a reply MOVE, successful receiver installation commits the transfer.
A concurrent revoke does not replace that committed result with
.Er EBADF
while source consumption is still pending.
The client waits for the server to consume the source descriptor and
acknowledge MOVE before CALL returns successfully.
The server waits for receiver installation success or failure even if the
endpoint was revoked after reply publication.
.Pp
The receiver sees
.Dv DOOR_DESCRIPTOR ,
not
.Dv DOOR_RELEASE .
Thus RELEASE is an instruction attached to the send operation, not persistent
metadata on the received capability.
The returned
.Va arg->desc_ptr
array from
.Fn door_call
is allocated by libdoor.
The caller must close each received descriptor it does not retain and then
pass the array to
.Xr free 3 .
.Pp
Descriptor transfer preserves the underlying capability type and rights.
It does not grant rights absent from a Capsicum-limited source descriptor and
does not bypass prison or
.Dv DOOR_CROSS_JAIL
policy.
.Ss Lifetime and information
.Fn door_revoke
revokes a Door owned by the process and wakes affected clients and workers.
.Fn door_info
returns its owner, callback/cookie handles for local Doors, attributes, and
stable generation identifier.
.Pp
.Fn door_bind
and
.Fn door_unbind
manage an explicit worker binding for the calling thread.
.Fn door_cred
and
.Fn door_ucred
return the current client's captured credentials only inside a normal callback.
.Fn door_getparam
and
.Fn door_setparam
access
.Dv DOOR_PARAM_DESC_MAX ,
.Dv DOOR_PARAM_DATA_MAX ,
and
.Dv DOOR_PARAM_DATA_MIN .
.Ss Core function reference
.Bl -tag -width Fn
.It Fn door_create
Validates the attributes, creates the endpoint, registers the callback and
cookie, and synchronously establishes the initial managed worker.
.It Fn door_call
Invokes an ordinary Door and waits for completion.
Request memory and descriptor arrays need remain valid only until return.
.Fa arg
may be
.Dv NULL
for an empty request whose result is intentionally discarded, as permitted by
the Solaris/illumos interface.
If the server nevertheless returns data or descriptors, libdoor releases the
replacement mapping and closes the received descriptors before returning
success.
On success reply data occupies
.Va rbuf
and returned descriptors occupy a separately allocated array.
If the reply exceeds
.Va rsize ,
the original buffer is left unchanged and a sufficiently large anonymous
mapping is returned through
.Va rbuf
and
.Va rsize .
The callback is executed exactly once; replacement-buffer allocation never
replays the RPC.
On failure, libdoor leaves the visible request fields unchanged.
The server may already have performed its operation and MOVE may have
committed before a later library allocation failure; retry can repeat effects.
.It Fn door_oneway
Submits one data-only request to a
.Dv DOOR_ONEWAY
endpoint.
Success means that the kernel snapshotted and accepted the bytes, not that the
server callback has completed.
.It Fn door_return
Completes the callback currently executing on the calling worker.
Reply objects are consumed synchronously before return.
After success the callback must return normally and must not return a second
time for that invocation.
.It Fn door_revoke
Prevents new calls and wakes affected clients and workers.
Previously issued descriptors remain descriptors but operations through them
observe revocation.
.It Fn door_info
Fills
.Vt door_info_t .
With
.Dv DOOR_QUERY
it reports the current worker binding.
Procedure and cookie handles are meaningful as process-local pointers only
when
.Dv DOOR_LOCAL
is set.
.It Fn door_cred , Fn door_ucred
Return compact or extended credentials captured for the current normal
callback.
The object returned by
.Fn door_ucred
is owned by libdoor and remains callback-local.
.It Fn door_bind , Fn door_unbind
Explicitly associate or disassociate the calling thread with a worker pool.
Ordinary applications normally use workers managed by libdoor.
.It Fn door_getparam , Fn door_setparam
Read or change a supported per-endpoint data or descriptor limit.
.It Fn door_server_create
Atomically installs the legacy process-wide depletion factory and returns its
predecessor.
.It Fn door_xcreate
Creates a private endpoint with application-provided worker construction.
Every created thread must execute the supplied start entry point exactly once.
.El
.Ss Pathname publication
.Fn fattach
publishes a live Door at an existing regular-file pathname using
.Xr namefs 4 .
Repeating it on the same publication rebinds new opens to a new generation.
.Fn fdetach
removes the exact namefs mount while preserving already-open capabilities.
.Ss Persistent shared memory
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_create "int fd" "size_t size" "uint_t flags" "door_shm_t *shm"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_attach "int fd" "door_shm_id_t region" "uint_t flags" "door_shm_t *shm"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_unmap "door_shm_t *shm"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_destroy "int fd" "door_shm_id_t region"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ref_init "const door_shm_t *shm" "size_t offset" "size_t size" "door_shm_ref_t *ref"
.Ft int
.Fn door_call_shm "int fd" "const door_shm_ref_t *ref"
.Ft int
.Fn door_call_shm_region "const door_shm_t *shm" "size_t offset" "size_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_destroy_handle "door_shm_t *shm"
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_create
creates and maps a generation-owned persistent region.
.Fn door_shm_attach
maps it into the owner/server process.
The exact requested size is authoritative even though VM mappings are rounded
to pages.
.Dv DOOR_SHM_CONSUMER_RO
permanently restricts the consumer mapping to read-only access.
.Pp
.Fn door_shm_ref_init
constructs a generation-qualified, bounds-checked reference.
.Fn door_call_shm
invokes the Door using that region, offset, and length without copying the
payload through the call ABI.
Destroying a handle invalidates future references but does not implicitly
unmap existing mappings.
.Fn door_shm_unmap
removes only the local mapping and closes libdoor's private descriptor which
pins the Door generation.
.Fn door_shm_destroy_handle
destroys through the strong handle and invalidates its region id on success.
Its mapping, size and private fd remain until
.Fn door_shm_unmap .
.Fn door_call_shm_region
combines bounds-checked reference construction and invocation.
All ranges are checked against the exact logical size, not merely the
page-rounded VM mapping.
.Ss Page-composite SHM views
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_publish "int fd" "const door_shm_ref_t *segments" "uint_t count" "door_shm_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_attach "int fd" "uint64_t id" "uint64_t token" "door_shm_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_query "door_shm_view_t *view" "uint_t *state"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_release "door_shm_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_destroy "door_shm_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_view_close "door_shm_view_t *view"
.Pp
Publish lends up to 64 nonoverlapping, page-aligned ranges of existing regions
from one Door generation. Only their creator can publish, and only the Door
owner can attach. Attach aliases the pages consecutively in segment order,
without copying payload bytes, with read-only maximum protection.
Overlapping active loans and source destroy return
.Er EBUSY .
Disjoint subranges can be lent concurrently.
.Pp
The producer retains its writable alias and must stop CPU/DMA writes and
recycling until release. The consumer must stop all readers before release,
which removes its mapping before returning source loans. Do not alter mapping
inheritance, create extra aliases, or share a live view through vfork/RFMEM.
The initial mapping is not inherited by normal fork.
.Pp
Revoke and CALL/async completion do not release an attached view.
Release remains available after revoke through the retained descriptor.
Late receiver exit/exec releases mappings; producer exit preserves attached
pages until receiver release. Canceling an unattached offer uses destroy;
destroy of a live attached offer returns
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
After release, close the consumer handle. Query the producer handle for
.Dv DOOR_SHM_VIEW_RELEASED
before recycling pages, and destroy it to close its descriptor and metadata.
Each handle owns a private descriptor. Do not independently copy handles or
pass them to
.Fn door_shm_unmap .
The API covers ordinary Door SHM, not device/netmap memory. Ring data must occupy
isolated whole pages and remain reserved until view release.
.Ss Checked SPSC ring
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_create "int fd" "size_t slot_size" "size_t slot_count" "door_shm_ring_t *ring"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_attach "int fd" "door_shm_id_t region" "uint_t role" "door_shm_ring_t *ring"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_reserve "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "door_shm_ring_writer_t *writer"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_commit "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "const door_shm_ring_writer_t *writer" "size_t size" "door_shm_ref_t *ref"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_abort "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "const door_shm_ring_writer_t *writer"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_push "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "const void *data" "size_t size" "door_shm_ref_t *ref"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_acquire "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "door_shm_ring_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_consume "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "const door_shm_ring_view_t *view"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_pop "door_shm_ring_t *ring" "void *data" "size_t capacity" "size_t *size"
.Ft int
.Fn door_shm_ring_unmap "door_shm_ring_t *ring"
.Pp
The ring is single-producer/single-consumer.
.Fn door_shm_ring_reserve
returns the next mutable slot;
.Fn door_shm_ring_commit
publishes only its committed prefix; and
.Fn door_shm_ring_abort
discards it.
.Fn door_shm_ring_acquire
returns a logical read view under the cooperating SPSC protocol, valid until
.Fn door_shm_ring_consume .
The copying wrappers are
.Fn door_shm_ring_push
and
.Fn door_shm_ring_pop .
Writer/view checks validate the current mapping, slot, sequence, size and
reservation state; they do not directly compare a Door uniquifier.
Generation-qualified
.Vt door_shm_ref_t
references carry strong Door identity separately.
A writable peer can modify bytes outside the protocol; the view is not
protected by VM immutability.
.Pp
Only one reservation may be outstanding on a producer handle and only one view
on a consumer handle.
.Fn door_shm_ring_reserve
returns
.Er EAGAIN
when full and
.Er EBUSY
when a reservation is already outstanding.
.Fn door_shm_ring_commit
publishes exactly the requested prefix, never the unused tail of a reused
slot.
.Fn door_shm_ring_abort
releases an unpublished reservation.
.Fn door_shm_ring_acquire
returns
.Er EAGAIN
when empty, while
.Fn door_shm_ring_consume
advances only for the exact unmodified view most recently acquired.
.Fn door_shm_ring_pop
reports the required size and leaves the item available if the destination is
too small.
.Fn door_shm_ring_unmap
releases the local mapping after outstanding local state has been resolved.
.Ss Optional named services
.Ft int
.Fn door_named_register "int fd" "const char *name" "uint_t flags"
.Ft int
.Fn door_named_unregister "int fd" "const char *name" "uint_t flags"
.Ft int
.Fn door_named_lookup "const char *name" "uint_t flags" "door_named_info_t *info"
.Ft int
.Fn door_named_connect "const char *name" "uint_t flags" "door_named_info_t *info"
.Pp
These functions use
.Pa /dev/doorctl ,
which exists only when the kernel includes
.Cd "options DOORCTL" .
Registration may be local to a prison or use
.Dv DOOR_NAMED_GLOBAL .
GLOBAL is accepted only for register/unregister. Lookup/connect require
flags zero and select local scope before global; lookup does not reserve a
generation for a later connect. Connect reports generation/jail identity and
sets dni_attributes to zero; lookup returns attributes.
Connect returns an ordinary descriptor pinned to the generation current at
that instant.
Unregistering a name does not invalidate capabilities already issued.
.Ss Worker factories
.Ft door_server_func_t *
.Fn door_server_create "door_server_func_t *factory"
.Ft int
.Fn door_xcreate "door_server_procedure_t *proc" "void *cookie" "uint_t attributes" "door_xcreate_server_func_t *factory" "door_xcreate_thrsetup_func_t *setup" "void *setup_arg" "int nthreads"
.Pp
.Fn door_server_create
installs the process-wide legacy depletion callback.
.Fn door_xcreate
creates a private Door whose worker lifecycle is supplied by the application.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions returning
.Vt int
return zero on success unless documented as returning a descriptor.
Descriptor-producing functions return a non-negative descriptor.
On failure, -1 is returned and
.Va errno
is set.
.Fn door_server_create
returns the previous callback.
.Sh ERRORS
Common errors include:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An argument, flag, role, lifecycle token, or callback context is invalid.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The descriptor, Door, generation, or region is invalid or revoked.
.It Bq Er EACCES
Mode, jail or mapping policy denies the operation.
.It Bq Er EPERM
Ownership or publication scope denies the operation.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A supplied memory range is inaccessible.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
No ring slot/item is available, or an async context, process or completion
Door admission limit is reached.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
A writer/view is already outstanding or another lifecycle operation conflicts.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
A reply/receive buffer, async payload or ring-slot size is exceeded.
An undersized ordinary CALL reply buffer is replaced automatically and is not
by itself an
.Er EMSGSIZE
condition.
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
A request violates the per-Door data-size limits.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
A request exceeds the per-Door descriptor limit.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A cancellable synchronous call was interrupted.
.It Bq Er ECANCELED
Explicit async cancellation won, or close drained a pending completion.
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
The assigned worker disappeared without endpoint revocation.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A named service or persistent region does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOTSUP
The requested attribute combination or credential operation is unsupported,
or descriptor transfer is forbidden by endpoint policy.
.El
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Independent Door descriptors and calls are thread-safe.
The checked ring requires exactly one producer and one consumer; a single ring
handle must not be used concurrently by multiple producers or consumers.
Callback-local credential state must not be retained after the callback.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr door 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr doorfs 4 ,
.Xr namefs 4 ,
.Xr jail 8
.Sh HISTORY
The public API follows the illumos Doors programming model and adds FreeBSD
extensions for ONEWAY calls, persistent shared memory, checked SPSC rings,
Capsicum, jails, and the optional named registry.
.Sh CAVEATS
Virtual addresses in
.Vt door_shm_t
are process-local and must never be sent to another process.
A writable shared region is intentionally shared state, not a mutual trust
boundary; validate application-level message formats even though kernel range
and generation checks succeed.
